//! check: fail(E1002)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.str.view.lend
//!
//! s89's soundness guard (wolf-lang#86), turned around by s92 (#107).
//! `s.bytes()` in an argument position offers the string's own storage
//! as a LEND — that is what makes the call free — and a lend that
//! outlives the call it was scoped to would be a dangling `{ptr, len}`.
//!
//! `keep` returns its parameter, so the bytes would leave with it.
//! Through s91 the checker REFUSED this (E1015). But the compiler had a
//! safe compilation the whole time — materialize the bytes into a
//! `List[byte]` at the call, bit-for-bit what every call did before views
//! crossed calls, and exactly what it already does for a callee whose
//! use it cannot classify. Refusing a program with a safe compilation
//! is not this language's style (#108: E1015 was the only rule in the
//! static-conservatism class doing so), so the bytes are copied and the
//! program runs.
//!
//! This is a better witness than the refusal was: it proves the
//! fallback WORKS (the program runs and prints `4`) rather than that
//! the checker fires. Both implementations run it now — lupin always
//! did — so the file stops being a divergence at all.
//!
//! s165 (wolf-lang#366) turned it around again, from the callee's side.
//! `keep` returning its `read` parameter is now E1002 in its own right:
//! the caller still holds what `keep` hands back, so the value would
//! have two live paths whether it arrived as a lend or as a copy. Every
//! escape the lend analysis can PROVE — a return, a `take` or `mut`
//! hand-on, a store past the call — is now that refusal or E1014.
//!
//! s167 (wolf-lang#387) retired W1004 on the strength of that: it fired
//! beside an error for one root cause (VOICE rule 5), and on the one
//! shape #366 leaves legal it claimed something untrue. So this file
//! pins the refusal alone, and the copy the lend degrades to is silent
//! again — which is what it was before s89 and what it always compiled
//! to.
//!
//! Its sibling `corpus/strings/byte_view_lend.lu` is the free half: the
//! same call shape, with callees that only read, and no warning.

fn keep(bs: List[byte]) -> List[byte] {
    bs                             // ERROR E1002: the caller still holds `bs`
}

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = "wolf"
    let held = keep(s.bytes())     // the lend degrades to a copy, silently
    print("{held.len}")
    0
}
